{
  "requirement": "Sending a previously prepared result again during a provider retry or a later conversation turn leaves the tool’s `calls` and `result_bytes` unchanged.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: `requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.ToolUsage.replay` has one clause: \"Sending a previously prepared result again during a provider retry or a later conversation turn leaves the tool's `calls` and `result_bytes` unchanged.\" This describes observable behavior only: the counters stay the same when a result is resent. It names no library, algorithm, signature, type, field layout, or delegation through a named interface.\n\nI checked whether any part of it prescribes structure. The `calls` and `result_bytes` counters are given as the observable values that must not change. Code could keep them in a different type, or deduplicate by a different mechanism such as an id set, a flag on the prepared result, or counting at preparation time. All of these give the same results and satisfy the text. So the implementation choice is discretionary.\n\nNo implementation roots were supplied, and the requirement has no `Implements` edges. No structural obligation needs implementation evidence here. Tests cover the behavior.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement has one behavioral obligation. When a prepared tool result is sent again, either during a provider retry or on a later conversation turn, the tool's `calls` and `result_bytes` must stay unchanged.\n\n`crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#fn[tool_usage_counts_utf8_results_and_errors_once_across_retries]` covers both cases:\n- **Retry:** The first provider turn returns three tool calls: two `read_node` and one `lookup`. The `Scenario` provider then returns a throttled error for the next request. That request carries the prepared results, so the retry sends them again. The final turn is another successful tool-call turn, which also sends the earlier results again as history.\n- **Later turn:** The final assertion requires `read_node` to be exactly `{calls:2, result_bytes:1280}`, `lookup` to be `{1, 6}` and `submit_result` to be `{1, 0}`. If a re-send had been counted again, these totals would be larger.\n\n`generic_conversations_can_use_default_telemetry_through_retry_and_continuation` adds a second scenario with a retry, a `FinishReason::Length` continuation and a later turn. It asserts `lookup.calls == 1`, that `lookup.result_bytes` equals the `\"fixture answer\"` length, and that `submit_result` stays at 1 call and 0 bytes. It also asserts that `session.dispatched == 1`. The final request history contains the earlier result, so the prepared result was sent again without changing the counts.\n\nBoth tests carry `{test}` references to `ToolUsage.replay`, and I found no gap.",
  "references": "f209268610aed44268109e4f0594d295fd446e0770b80b41bca82898be502af0",
